Beats me. Now...

274
00:12:47,300 --> 00:12:48,590
I know Hawking's not busy

275
00:12:48,590 --> 00:12:51,640
because I can see he's playing other people right now.

276
00:12:51,640 --> 00:12:53,650
Maybe since you're so good,

277
00:12:53,650 --> 00:12:56,210
he's taking his time to meet the challenge.

278
00:12:56,210 --> 00:12:57,700
I want Penny to enjoy school...

279
00:12:57,700 --> 00:12:59,950
Wolowitz told me he's a big baby!

280
00:12:59,950 --> 00:13:01,350
But I didn't know that,

281
00:13:01,350 --> 00:13:03,470
and I played "Extract" for 82 points.

282
00:13:03,470 --> 00:13:05,760
It's all Amy's fault. She told me to play it.

283
00:13:05,760 --> 00:13:07,690
I have got to cut her loose.

284
00:13:09,250 --> 00:13:11,200
Sheldon, I wasn't done talking.

285
00:13:11,670 --> 00:13:13,250
She hands in the paper tomorrow.

286
00:13:13,250 --> 00:13:15,180
I know I could help her.

287
00:13:15,180 --> 00:13:16,610
And she's my girlfriend...

288
00:13:16,610 --> 00:13:18,410
I-I should be allowed to help her.

289
00:13:18,410 --> 00:13:19,850
Why aren't I allowed to help her?

290
00:13:19,850 --> 00:13:21,460
Yeah, I hear you, brother.

291
00:13:22,740 --> 00:13:23,730
No.

292
00:13:24,110 --> 00:13:25,450
You need to give me some advice.

293
00:13:25,450 --> 00:13:27,360
Fine. Women, huh?

294
00:13:27,360 --> 00:13:28,090
No...

295
00:13:31,020 --> 00:13:33,080
Specific to my situation.

296
00:13:33,080 --> 00:13:34,720
Blonde women, huh?

297
00:13:37,340 --> 00:13:38,450
Empathetic!

298
00:13:38,450 --> 00:13:40,930
- It sucks to be you. - I quit.

299
00:13:41,180 --> 00:13:43,260
Leonard, wait. No.

300
00:13:43,260 --> 00:13:45,470
I listened to your dumb thing.

301
00:13:46,490 --> 00:13:47,620
Leonard, come back.

302
00:13:47,620 --> 00:13:48,810
Leonard, come back.

303
00:13:48,810 --> 00:13:50,210
Leonard, come back.

304
00:13:50,210 --> 00:13:51,520
- What? - Oh, of course,

305
00:13:51,520 --> 00:13:53,450
it only works on the weak-minded.

306
00:14:03,070 --> 00:14:05,510
Good morning, sunshine.

307
00:14:06,260 --> 00:14:07,830
Leonard.

308
00:14:07,830 --> 00:14:09,560
It's 8:00 a.M.

309
00:14:09,570 --> 00:14:11,910
It's like the middle of the night.

310
00:14:13,110 --> 00:14:14,940
I know, but I have to go to work,

311
00:14:14,940 --> 00:14:16,870
and I made you breakfast.

312
00:14:16,870 --> 00:14:18,770
Oh, wow, that's so sweet.

313
00:14:18,770 --> 00:14:21,080
- Hey, what's this? - Uh, before you open that,

314
00:14:21,080 --> 00:14:23,980
um, are you familiar with the story of

315
00:14:23,980 --> 00:14:25,270
"The Shoemaker and the Elves"?

316
00:14:25,270 --> 00:14:26,530
Elves? Come on, Leonard.

317
00:14:26,530 --> 00:14:29,620
- It's too early for Lord of the Rings. - No, no.

318
00:14:32,790 --> 00:14:33,740
Listen, um...

319
00:14:33,740 --> 00:14:35,630
Once upon a time, there was this shoemaker

320
00:14:35,630 --> 00:14:37,450
and when he went to bed at night,

321
00:14:37,450 --> 00:14:38,830
elves would sneak in

322
00:14:38,830 --> 00:14:42,100
and they would make all these amazing shoes for him.

323
00:14:42,230 --> 00:14:43,930
And when the shoemaker woke up in the morning,

324
00:14:43,930 --> 00:14:46,130
he-he would be super happy,

325
00:14:46,140 --> 00:14:49,140
not mad at the elves at all.

326
00:14:50,010 --> 00:14:51,810
Open it.

327
00:14:52,340 --> 00:14:54,030
Okay.

328
00:14:54,030 --> 00:14:56,740
"An examination of the economic, cultural,

329
00:14:56,740 --> 00:14:58,540
and political roots of slavery

330
00:14:58,540 --> 00:15:03,090
in the Old South: 1619 to 1865."

331
00:15:03,910 --> 00:15:05,650
What the hell is this?

332
00:15:06,370 --> 00:15:08,790
Don't ask me. A little elf did it.

333
00:15:11,310 --> 00:15:12,950
So let me get this straight.

334
00:15:12,950 --> 00:15:15,180
You just assumed my paper would be bad

335
00:15:15,180 --> 00:15:16,550
so you wrote one for me?

336
00:15:16,550 --> 00:15:18,440
No, I assumed it would be good. Then I read it.

337
00:15:18,440 --> 00:15:20,340
- What?! - No, I...

338
00:15:21,280 --> 00:15:22,340
I mean, it was good.

339
00:15:22,340 --> 00:15:24,610
There were just a few things that needed a little polishing.

340
00:15:24,610 --> 00:15:27,920
- W... You changed every word. - That's not true.

341
00:15:29,100 --> 00:15:30,340
"Slavery."

342
00:15:32,600 --> 00:15:34,470
"1619,"

343
00:15:34,470 --> 00:15:36,900
your name at the top-- that's all you.

344
00:15:37,850 --> 00:15:39,690
You are such an ass.

345
00:15:39,690 --> 00:15:41,190
This is exactly why I didn't want to tell you

346
00:15:41,190 --> 00:15:42,990
I was taking a class in the first place.

347
00:15:42,990 --> 00:15:44,690
Please don't be upset. I-I just...

348
00:15:44,690 --> 00:15:46,930
I didn't want you ending up with a bad grade

349
00:15:46,930 --> 00:15:48,900
and get discouraged and give up on the idea

350
00:15:48,900 --> 00:15:50,100
of going back to school.

351
00:15:50,100 --> 00:15:52,230
Right, because me being in school

352
00:15:52,230 --> 00:15:53,730
is so important to you.

353
00:15:53,740 --> 00:15:55,940
That way, you wouldn't have to be dating someone

354
00:15:55,940 --> 00:15:57,310
who's only a waitress.

355
00:15:57,310 --> 00:15:58,940
Oh, come on, you know that's not true.

356
00:15:58,940 --> 00:16:00,480
Do I? Listen to me.

357
00:16:00,480 --> 00:16:02,140
I need to do this on my own.

358
00:16:02,140 --> 00:16:04,280
If I fail, I fail. If I pass, I pass.

359
00:16:04,280 --> 00:16:05,350
Do you get it?

360
00:16:05,820 --> 00:16:07,070
I'm sorry. I was just trying to help.

361
00:16:07,070 --> 00:16:09,040
Yeah, well, next time don't.

362
00:16:09,920 --> 00:16:12,020
Oh, and since you like stories so much,

363
00:16:12,020 --> 00:16:14,150
this is not "The Shoemaker and the Elves," okay?

364
00:16:14,150 --> 00:16:16,260
This is, "Give a man a fish, he eats it.

365
00:16:16,260 --> 00:16:17,710
Teach a man to fish, he...

366
00:16:17,710 --> 00:16:19,710
sells it or something." Whatever, I don't know!

367
00:16:19,710 --> 00:16:22,060
It's just a lot better than what you did, you big jerk!

368
00:16:28,290 --> 00:16:30,090
You ever hear back from Hawking?

369
00:16:30,580 --> 00:16:31,850
No.

370
00:16:31,850 --> 00:16:34,140
It would appear as if I've lost him.

371
00:16:34,140 --> 00:16:36,130
Stupid brain.

372
00:16:38,240 --> 00:16:39,440
It'll be okay.

373
00:16:39,440 --> 00:16:41,210
How can it be okay?

374
00:16:41,210 --> 00:16:42,860
Stephen Hawking's a genius

375
00:16:42,860 --> 00:16:44,730
and he talks like a robot.

376
00:16:44,730 --> 00:16:47,520
It's everything I've ever wanted in a friend.

377
00:16:48,690 --> 00:16:50,160
Yeah, but if he's a sore loser,

378
00:16:50,160 --> 00:16:51,760
maybe you're better off without him.

379
00:16:52,740 --> 00:16:54,350
You're right.

380
00:16:54,350 --> 00:16:57,260
I guess I just have to make lemonade out of the two of you.

381
00:17:01,460 --> 00:17:03,540
He played! He played!

382
00:17:03,540 --> 00:17:06,830
Coop and Rolling Thunder are together again.

383
00:17:08,010 --> 00:17:10,410
He was okay with that nickname.

384
00:17:11,220 --> 00:17:13,490
So, now all you have to do is let him win.

385
00:17:13,490 --> 00:17:14,800
Way ahead of you.

386
00:17:14,800 --> 00:17:17,280
I will play the word "At"

387
00:17:17,280 --> 00:17:20,050
for two measly points, throwing the game

388
00:17:20,050 --> 00:17:21,560
and thus securing my friendship

389
00:17:21,560 --> 00:17:23,550
with the smartest man in the world.

390
00:17:31,630 --> 00:17:33,200
What are you waiting for? Hit "Send."

391
00:17:33,200 --> 00:17:34,470
I can't.

392
00:17:34,470 --> 00:17:37,190
Losing on purpose is intellectually dishonest.

393
00:17:37,190 --> 00:17:38,130
So don't do it.

394
00:17:38,130 --> 00:17:40,040
Oh, but I want to be Hawking's friend.

395
00:17:40,040 --> 00:17:41,340
So do it.

396
00:17:41,340 --> 00:17:43,280
No, but if I do, I'll be a phony,

397
00:17:43,280 --> 00:17:44,410
a sellout,

398
00:17:44,410 --> 00:17:46,170
a Hollywood poser.

399
00:17:47,550 --> 00:17:49,350
Then don't do it.

400
00:17:50,060 --> 00:17:51,460
I won't.

401
00:17:51,460 --> 00:17:52,950
This feels right.

402
00:17:52,950 --> 00:17:55,810
My mother always said, "to thine own self be true."

403
00:17:55,810 --> 00:17:56,920
Good for you.

404
00:17:56,920 --> 00:17:58,630
'Course she also told me that every animal in the world

405
00:17:58,630 --> 00:18:01,710
got on one boat, so what does she know? And send.

406
00:18:08,870 --> 00:18:10,200
Haven't heard from you in a couple days.

407
00:18:10,210 --> 00:18:11,210
You still mad at me?

408
00:18:11,210 --> 00:18:12,440
Nope.

409
00:18:12,440 --> 00:18:14,510
I have no reason to...

410
00:18:14,510 --> 00:18:16,470
"B" mad at you.

411
00:18:21,880 --> 00:18:23,650
That's right. On my paper.

412
00:18:23,650 --> 00:18:26,380
Not yours, mine, you punk-ass elf.

413
00:18:28,800 --> 00:18:30,390
I don't know what to say.

414
00:18:30,390 --> 00:18:31,840
Hmm, how about,

415
00:18:31,840 --> 00:18:34,610
"Gee, Penny, you're smarter than I thought.

416
00:18:34,610 --> 00:18:36,240
You may be the one in school,

417
00:18:36,240 --> 00:18:38,330
but I'm the one who learned a lesson.

418
00:18:38,330 --> 00:18:42,100
I'm so stupid, Penny. Duh..."

419
00:18:47,180 --> 00:18:50,140
She sounds exactly like you!

420
00:18:52,180 --> 00:18:54,250
How'd it go with Leonard?

421
00:18:54,250 --> 00:18:57,350
I don't think he'll be making that mistake again.

422
00:18:57,350 --> 00:18:58,390
Good.

423
00:18:58,910 --> 00:19:00,450
So, I know this goes without saying,

424
00:19:00,450 --> 00:19:01,820
but if either of you tell Leonard

425
00:19:01,820 --> 00:19:03,020
you helped me rewrite this paper,

426
00:19:03,030 --> 00:19:05,270
I will beat you both with a bag of oranges.

427
00:19:06,530 --> 00:19:08,010
- Got it. - Understood.

428
00:19:08,010 --> 00:19:10,650
Now, ladies, we got a B-minus on this paper.

429
00:19:10,650 --> 00:19:12,300
I think if we put our heads together,

430
00:19:12,300 --> 00:19:14,570
on the next one we could get an "A."

431
00:19:14,570 --> 00:19:17,540
Uh, but we got you a B-minus on purpose to make it believable.

432
00:19:17,540 --> 00:19:18,740
Believable?

433
00:19:19,910 --> 00:19:21,280
You saying I'm not smart?

434
00:19:21,280 --> 00:19:22,740
- No, no! - You're smart.

435
00:19:24,910 --> 00:19:26,290
That's better.

436
00:19:28,370 --> 00:19:30,160
I feel like I'm in high school again.

437
00:19:30,160 --> 00:19:34,100
Yeah, doing the prom queen's homework so she'll like us.

438
00:19:34,490 --> 00:19:36,000
I know.

439
00:19:36,490 --> 00:19:38,650
It's finally working.

440
00:19:49,930 --> 00:19:51,400
It's Stephen Hawking.

441
00:19:51,920 --> 00:19:54,500
Answer it. I want to hear.

442
00:19:56,520 --> 00:19:57,840
Professor Hawking,

443
00:19:57,840 --> 00:19:59,570
how nice of you to call.

444
00:19:59,570 --> 00:20:00,600
Hello.

445
00:20:00,600 --> 00:20:03,620
I really enjoyed our game, Dr. Cooper.

446
00:20:03,620 --> 00:20:06,280
Oh, me, too.

447
00:20:06,280 --> 00:20:09,110
Or should I say Dr. Loser?

448
00:20:12,420 --> 00:20:13,750
Ha, ha, ha.

449
00:20:16,440 --> 00:20:18,540
Yes, congratulations.

450
00:20:18,770 --> 00:20:20,510
You won fair and square.

451
00:20:20,510 --> 00:20:22,600
Uh, very impressive, sir.

452
00:20:22,600 --> 00:20:24,580
Do you like brain teasers?

453
00:20:24,580 --> 00:20:27,400
Oh, I love brain teasers.

454
00:20:27,400 --> 00:20:29,370
What does Sheldon Cooper

455
00:20:29,370 --> 00:20:32,750
and a black hole have in common?

456
00:20:33,040 --> 00:20:34,930
They both suck.

457
00:20:42,210 --> 00:20:44,270
Neener neener.

